---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, work } from '../data/site';
---

<Base title="About" description={site.tagline}>
  <section class="wrap section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Logbook · About</p>
    <h1>About</h1>

    <div class="prose bio">
      <p>
        I’m a product person, a builder, a tinkerer, and someone who is still figuring things out.
      </p>
      <p>
        The most important part of my life is my family. They keep me grounded, remind me what actually matters,
        and probably tolerate far more of my random ideas and experiments than they should. Everything else I do is,
        in some way, shaped by the kind of life and person I want to build around them.
      </p>
      <p>
      I’ve always enjoyed making things. Sometimes that means software—building a little product, writing some code,
      experimenting with AI, or trying to automate something that probably didn't need automating. Other times it means 
      hardware: taking something apart to understand how it works, tinkering with electronics, or attempting to build 
      something simply because I’m curious whether I can.
      </p>
      <p>
      That curiosity eventually found a natural home in Product Management. I enjoy sitting at the intersection of people,
      technology, and problems that aren't always clearly defined. I like asking why something works the way it does,
      questioning assumption and useful. What I enjoy most isn't shipping features for the sake of shipping them—it's 
      discovering something that matters and figuring out how to make it meaningfully better.
      </p>
      <p>
      Outside of work, you'll probably find me playing a racket sport. Badminton and table tennis are my favourites. I'm 
      competitive enough to care about winning, but not competitive enough to stop playing after losing. I like the 
      combination of instinct, strategy, repetition, and those tiny improvements that only become visible after you've 
      played for a while.
      </p>
      <p>
      I'm also fascinated by human psychology. Why do people behave the way they do? Why do we make decisions that 
      don't always make rational sense? What makes us trust someone, change our minds, form habits, or become
      attached to something? A lot of this curiosity naturally finds its way into how I think about products, 
      but I'm equally interested in people simply because people are complicated.
      </p>
      <p>
      So this website is a little collection of the things that occupy my head: products I'm building, problems 
      I'm thinking about, things I'm learning, things I'm tinkering with, and occasionally things I find interesting 
      for no particularly good reason.
      </p>

      
    </div>

    <hr class="divider timeline-rule" />

    <h2 class="tl-heading">Where I've worked</h2>
    <ol class="timeline">
      {
        work.map((role) => (
          <li class="tl-item">
            <p class="meta tl-period">{role.period}</p>
            <div class="tl-body">
              <h3 class="tl-title">
                {role.title} · <span class="tl-org">{role.org}</span>
              </h3>
              <p class="tl-desc">{role.description}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </section>

  <style>
    .bio {
      margin-top: 1.5rem;
      font-size: 1.1rem;
    }
    .timeline-rule {
      margin-block: 2.5rem;
    }
    .tl-heading {
      margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }
    .timeline {
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 0;
    }
    .tl-item {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 10rem 1fr;
      gap: 1.2rem;
      padding-block: 1.4rem;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }
    .tl-period {
      padding-top: 0.3rem;
    }
    .tl-org {
      color: var(--accent-ink);
      font-family: var(--font-body);
    }
    .tl-desc {
      color: var(--ink-soft);
      margin-top: 0.4rem;
    }
    @media (max-width: 560px) {
      .tl-item {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
        gap: 0.4rem;
      }
    }
  </style>
</Base>
